{
  "dom_id": "question-325132",
  "name": null,
  "problem": "The daily commute time for employees is normally distributed with an average of $40$ minutes and an unknown standard deviation. Tests show that $15%$ of commutes take more than $52$ minutes. Assuming a normal model, what percentage of commutes take less than $31.7$ minutes? **Round the answer to $1$ decimal place.**\n\n*Hint: A table of values of ${Φ}^{-1}(p)$ for different values of $p$ is given below, where $p=Φ(z)$ is the cumulative distribution function for the standard normal distribution. *\n\n| $\\,p\\,$ | $0.75$ | $0.80$ | $0.85$ | $0.90$ | $0.95$ | $0.97$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| ${Φ}^{-1}(p)$ | $\\,0.6745\\,$ | $\\,0.8416\\,$ | $\\,1.0364\\,$ | $\\,1.2816\\,$ | $\\,1.6449\\,$ | $\\,1.8808\\,$ |\n\n*In addition, you're given the following table of values for $p=Φ(z).$*\n\n| $z$ | $\\,0.02\\,$ | $\\,0.03\\,$ | $\\,0.04\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\,-0.7\\,$ | $\\,0.2358\\,$ | $\\,0.2327\\,$ | $\\,0.2296\\,$ |\n| $\\,-0.6\\,$ | $\\,0.2676\\,$ | $\\,0.2643\\,$ | $\\,0.2611\\,$ |\n| $\\,-0.5\\,$ | $\\,0.3015\\,$ | $\\,0.2981\\,$ | $\\,0.2946\\,$ |\n\nApproximately\n{{field-1}}$%$ of commutes take less than $31.7$ minutes.",
  "worked_solution": "",
